When sending a scanned document containing sensitive information like your Social Security Number (SSN) over email or other potentially unsecured channels, it is highly recommended to redact it to protect your identity. 

Here are a few general guidelines to follow:

1. **Check the Requirements:** Ask the recipient if they actually need the full SSN. In many cases, organizations only need the last four digits for verification purposes.
2. **Redact Appropriately:** If they only need the last four digits, completely black out or cover the first five digits before scanning the document. If you are redacting a digital file, make sure to use a proper redaction tool (simply drawing a black box over the text in a basic image editor can sometimes be undone).
3. **Use Secure Transmission:** If the recipient absolutely requires the full SSN, **do not send it via standard email**, as email is generally not encrypted end-to-end. Instead, ask if they have a secure, encrypted file-sharing portal, or consider sending the document via certified mail or fax.

Always err on the side of caution when handling Personally Identifiable Information (PII) to prevent identity theft.

